      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "What Does It Mean ‘Long-Range’?"
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "551218067f0f417533b6ae17498e17e45b51124adedd3428fddab53513fd02a2"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 34,
      "pptx_slide": 33,
      "title": "Random Walk",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Random Walk",
        "A random walk (RW) on 𝐺 is a Markov chain which, at each step, moves from a node 𝑣 to",
        "one of its neighbours with probability 1/𝑑3 .",
        "Commute time 𝜏 𝑣, 𝑢 is the expected",
        "number of steps in a RW starting at 𝑣",
        "to reach node 𝑢 and come back.",
        "Effective resistance (ER) is",
        "𝑅𝑒𝑠 𝑣, 𝑢 = 𝜏(𝑣, 𝑢)/2|𝐸|."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "b76f83e7735b1786d02ba32bc6810f5bb218a1bb2a9917ab2b575da96ffedbb9",
        "4906602f6a303a21e0c1e3504faebb7f3c2beb9778563f2dbe78d33d9b235dc3"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 35,
      "pptx_slide": 34,
      "title": "Over-Squashing and Commute Time",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Over-Squashing and Commute Time",
        "Given 𝐴 = 𝐷 '(𝐴𝐷 '(// and 𝜎 = ReLU:",
        "This has some important consequences:",
        "• Reducing the sensitivity to messages from nodes with high effective resistance (ER)",
        "can be beneficial when the task only depends on ‘local’ interactions.",
        "• Over-squashing is an issue if the task depends on the interaction of nodes at high ER.",
        "• The ER represents an obstruction which is independent of the number of layers."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "cc52f037221882a5fc3051f1dafb17269ffb3656483e5da764b2014453b92379",
        "c00209ca94af26e48ab047658a6bed04e7360050bf4cb88a29e61b575b8142be"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 36,
      "pptx_slide": 35,
      "title": "Towards Dynamic Graph Rewiring",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Towards Dynamic Graph Rewiring",
        "Add edges among each pair of nodes within a certain distance,",
        "then the new graph is much dense.",
        "• More sensitive to over-smoothing",
        "• Higher impact on training time",
        "• Loses inductive bias afforded by",
        "the distance."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "e1e970e5fcfd96b3612caa10bbcf97e92e8b589e9b47f4f9b0001e917b428a4e",
        "36d970f6d963745b48b20c893fc2e3ef65544205cf964f452749f6d579ca3eef"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 37,
      "pptx_slide": 36,
      "title": "Dynamic Edge-Addition and Delay",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Dynamic Edge-Addition and Delay",
        "For classic MPNNs, information only travels from a node to its neighbours.",
        "• In DREW, adds edges connecting nodes at distance 𝑟.",
        "• In 𝜈-DREW, introduce delay depending on the distnace between nodes."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "4f846ac51ddea87cac2db1750a5d1efcfda91dda43692917b9531d6d8189603e",
        "24c2edd99adeb59a6d82abacb934b7b3238590b26e1ae6bba024397fbb7176d9"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 38,
      "pptx_slide": 37,
      "title": "𝜈-DREW in Equations",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "𝜈-DREW in Equations",
        "Introduce 𝜏: 𝑘 = max(0, 𝑘 − 𝜈) and consider the family of 𝜈-DREW-MPNN",
        "• No delay corresponds to 𝜈 → ∞.",
        "• The agg and com are parameterized using weights depending on the layer 𝑡 and the distance 𝑘."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "63d48c63ac936e694296a0b93e0aeb9e9450d42958e42deb850cc25cbe2bc3cc",
        "03393bcc46b9c26b60ed0e1549beb7e0ec6275d2e49ea29df0623c0675558b68"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 39,
      "pptx_slide": 38,
      "title": "Advantages of 𝜈-DREW",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Advantages of 𝜈-DREW",
        "The graph is progressively filled with each layer making for a more efficient framework.",
        "Preserves the inductive bias provided by the input grpah,",
        "since nodes that are closer still interact earlier.",
        "Mitigates over-smoothing: a larger delay means that 𝑣 aggregates the feature from 𝑢 before",
        "they are (significantly) ‘smoothed’ by repeated message passing."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "513eb4d46177717c574038a2084858aff2541cd66c30f93f8b5a9535abee4a60"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 40,
      "pptx_slide": 39,
      "title": "Long-Range Graph Benchmarks",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Long-Range Graph Benchmarks",
        "New graph benchmarks introduced with tasks likely to exhibit long-range dependencies.",
        "• Graph Transformers were shown to be superior to standard MPNNs."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "8daca8836d789f3be34fc33053f96fec838458cbe4eca7fa0865b90709a6a48e",
        "a289d7a54564b82ead48c36064d270108c5fc5e3be8a20dfebb6bbdf86bec323",
        "b8ae3a292344946424c408a56a286f55f352b6757e3827b044c8dfb8d6e1088d"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 41,
      "pptx_slide": 40,
      "title": "Long-Range Graph Benchmarks",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Long-Range Graph Benchmarks"
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "39bb1dc49b0c57ce1085e982bdd41a76afddd350c9313a055abe6718a38b4beb"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 42,
      "pptx_slide": 41,
      "title": "Critical Thinking for GNN Research",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Critical Thinking for GNN Research",
        "There is a plethora of GNN papers, but I provide warning for newcomers in reading them.",
        "Current GNN benchmarks are rather poor, so you should not",
        "always trust the empirical results!"
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "6bf2493ebe768d594e8e77ff21dfb0f833b15e2facb1cfd163db101a91d8680a"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 43,
      "pptx_slide": 42,
      "title": "Overview",
      "classification": "transition-only",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Overview"
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "b678f0afcb5071c4d8d4fec42aaf6d887816bac674b4068dc0bd2c2b8bab6452"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 44,
      "pptx_slide": 43,
      "title": "Missing Real-World Applications",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Missing Real-World Applications",
        "GNNs developed around niche sub-fileds,",
        "e.g., predicting properties from 2D molecular graph.",
        "Suggestions:",
        "• Shift focus to natural graph problems with real-world impact,",
        "e.g., combinatorial optimization or relational databases.",
        "• Improve conditional diffusion models for combinatorial tasks."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 45,
      "pptx_slide": 44,
      "title": "Graphs Lack Meaning",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Graphs Lack Meaning",
        "Graphs are often constructed artificially to test a hypothesis, not for practical applications.",
        "• “Not everything that could be modeled as a graph should be modeled as a graph.”",
        "Suggestions:",
        "• When proposing new benchmarks, discuss the advantage of graph structure",
        "• Benchmarks should include baselines with unstructured sets to ensure graph structure’s advantage."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [
        "679626a26af21c441f13df9646df6a2ee71bf7e37ebe138b9d3f631a72b45d94"
      ],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 46,
      "pptx_slide": 45,
      "title": "Bad Benchmarking Culture",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Bad Benchmarking Culture",
        "Often, datasets have no standard splits, eval, protocol, or detailed hyper-params.",
        "Researchers often report marginal gains w/o statistical significance.",
        "Suggestions :",
        "• The community should shift its focus beyond merely improving performance.",
        "• Propose practical tasks and robust evaluation frameworks."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 47,
      "pptx_slide": 46,
      "title": "No True Foundation Models",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "No True Foundation Models",
        "Impactful graph foundation models are yet to emerge.",
        "Even domain-specific graph-based foundation models only show marginal gains.",
        "Suggestions:",
        "• Shift paradigm from one model for one dataset to one model for all datasets.",
        "• Create large-scale, high-quality datasets of diverse graph structures."
      ],
      "media_sha256": [],
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "slide": 48,
      "pptx_slide": 47,
      "title": "Empirical Evidence",
      "classification": "substantive",
      "coverage": "pending",
      "text": [
        "Empirical Evidence",
        "Graphs are not necessarily constructed in a meaningful way."
      ],
